# Authz And Identity Model

Loom uses route classes and middleware to enforce access boundaries.

## Route classes

- **PublicRouter**: unauthenticated routes (health, provider discovery, auth entrypoints, docs search, webhook/ping surfaces).
- **AuthedRouter**: authenticated user routes (`/api/*` product surfaces).
- **Admin routes**: nested under `/api/admin`, protected by elevated role checks.
- **SCIM routes**: `/api/scim/*`, bearer-token protected for IdP provisioning.
- **Optional-auth routes**: mixed-access Git and browser route family.

## Identity providers

Server supports provider-backed login flows through configured OAuth clients:

- GitHub
- Google
- Okta
- device and magic-link related auth routes

## Session model

- Browser/session auth is managed server-side.
- CLI stores auth token and forwards it to server-backed APIs.
- ACP/editor integrations rely on authenticated server interaction through CLI agent pathways.

## Role boundaries

Typical boundaries include:

- end-user authenticated actions
- org-admin actions
- system-admin actions
- SCIM integrator token actions

